\f[CR]isIdentity\f[R]
.PP
Each property\(cqs value is copied as\-is.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Calling toJSON() directly
This example creates two \f[CR]DOMMatrixReadOnly\f[R] objects: a 2D
matrix and a 3D matrix.
Calling \f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] directly returns a plain object.
Its properties have the same values as the corresponding properties of
the original object.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] matrix = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMMatrixReadOnly(\(dqtranslate(20px, 30px)\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] matrix3D = \f[B]new\f[R] DOMMatrixReadOnly(\(dqtranslate3d(22px, 55px, 66px)\(dq);

\f[B]const\f[R] json = matrix.toJSON();
console.log(json); \f[I]// A plain object\f[R]
console.log(\f[B]typeof\f[R] json); \f[I]// \(dqobject\(dq\f[R]
console.log(json.is2D); \f[I]// Same value as matrix.is2D\f[R]
.EE
.SS Serializing to a JSON string
The \f[CR]DOMMatrixReadOnly\f[R] object from the previous example is
serialized using \f[CR]JSON.stringify()\f[R], which calls the
\f[CR]toJSON()\f[R] method automatically.
.IP
.EX
console.log(JSON.stringify(matrix));
.EE
.PP
This would log a JSON string like so (formatted for readability):
.IP
